<header><p class="st-kicker">other · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Awadh State</h1><p class="st-meta">1722 CE – 1856 CE · capital Lucknow</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The kingdom of the Nawabs of Awadh, whose courts at Faizabad and Lucknow were famed for refinement, until its British annexation helped spark 1857.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1722 the Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-shah-mughal-emperor">Muhammad Shah</a> appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saadat-ali-khan-i">Saadat Ali Khan</a>, a Shia noble from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nishapur">Nishapur</a>, as governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Awadh</a>. As Mughal power weakened, he and his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safdar-jang">Safdar Jang</a> made the governorship hereditary, ruling the rich middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges plain</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faizabad">Faizabad</a> and, from 1775, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucknow">Lucknow</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shuja-ud-daula">Shuja ud-Daula</a>'s defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-buxar">Buxar</a> in 1764 made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Awadh</a> a subsidiary ally of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">British East India Company</a>, which stationed troops at its expense. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asaf-ud-daula">Asaf ud-Daula</a> built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bara-imambara">Bara Imambara</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucknow">Lucknow</a>, and the court patronised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a> poetry, kathak dance, thumri music and a celebrated cuisine. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazi-ud-din-haidar-shah">Ghazi ud-Din Haidar</a> took the title of king in 1819 with British encouragement. Shia <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> was the court religion.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Governor-General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lord-dalhousie">Dalhousie</a> annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Awadh</a> in 1856, alleging misrule, and exiled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wajid-ali-shah">Wajid Ali Shah</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a>. Resentment among Awadhi soldiers and landholders fed the 1857 rebellion, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/begum-hazrat-mahal">Begum Hazrat Mahal</a> led resistance at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucknow">Lucknow</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/awadh-state.jpg" alt="Awadh State" width="320" height="251"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1857_Flag_of_Princely_state_of_Oudh_(Awadh),_during_the_siege_of_Lucknow_in_1857.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1722 CE – 1856 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lucknow</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Saadat Ali Khan, Asaf ud-Daula, Wajid Ali Shah</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Shia Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Persian, Urdu, Awadhi</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Oudh, Nawabs of Awadh, Oudh State, Nawabate of Awadh</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1789&amp;focus=awadh-state" aria-label="Awadh State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6844 1195 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Awadh State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7164 1451L7018 1385M7164 1451L7364 1601M7164 1451L7091 1429"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7018 1385h0M7364 1601h0M7091 1429h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7164 1451h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7164 1451h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/